Rayleigh scattering: SKY COLOR

FUNDAMENTAL
Rayleigh scattering: air molecules (mainly N2 and O2) scatter sunlight.
Shorter wavelengths scatter far more strongly than longer ones.

KEY RELATION
Scattering intensity I  ∝  1 / λ⁴

VARIABLES
λ   wavelength of light
I   scattered intensity
L   path length through atmosphere

TYPES OF LIGHT
Sunlight = mix of wavelengths (appears white)
Violet / Blue   short λ   high scatter
Green
Yellow
Orange / Red    long λ    low scatter

WHY THE SKY IS BLUE
Air molecules scatter blue and violet much more than red.
Scattered short-λ light reaches our eyes from all directions.
Sky therefore looks blue.

WHY SUNSETS ARE RED
When the Sun is low, light travels a much longer path (large L).
Most blue and violet is scattered away before it reaches you.
Remaining light is dominated by red and orange.

OTHER FACTORS
Ozone absorption (stronger at large angles)
Dust, haze, and pollution (Mie scattering)
Cleaner air → bluer skies, more vivid sunsets
More particles → redder, less clear colors
